<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Provably Safe, Yet Scalable
<br class="ltx_break">Reinforcement Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kai S. Yun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kaisyun@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zeyang Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zeyang@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Navid Azizan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">azizan@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Safe reinforcement learning (RL) aims to learn policies that optimize rewards while satisfying constraints.
Predominant approaches rely on soft-constrained policy optimization, which has achieved empirical success but does not provide formal safety guarantees for the learned policy.
In contrast, methods with strict guarantees typically rely on explicit certificate functions, whose construction requires the direct synthesis and verification of control-invariant sets, a process that scales poorly with state dimension and often yields overly conservative behavior.
In this paper, we present the Provably Safe, <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">yet</em> Scalable RL (PS2-RL) framework, a novel two-phase architecture for learning provably safe policies in a scalable manner, designed to overcome the key bottlenecks of prior methods.
Rather than explicitly computing invariant sets, PS2-RL leverages a learned backup policy to forward-integrate the system dynamics, generating an implicit control-invariant set online.
In the first phase, the backup policy is trained with our proposed safe-arrival value function, which characterizes the optimal backup policy for invariant-set construction.
In the second phase, an RL policy is trained end-to-end through a differentiable projection layer that strictly enforces the safety guarantees induced by the learned backup policy.
By maximizing the volume of the implicit control-invariant set in the first phase, the resulting PS2 policy from the second phase is performant and scalable, while maintaining provable safety.
Crucially, PS2-RL imposes no restrictions on the underlying RL algorithm and can be plugged into any existing training pipeline.
We establish theoretical guarantees for the proposed framework and evaluate it on robotic control tasks with state dimensions up to 10, a regime in which prior provably safe RL methods struggle or become impractical.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule ltx_align_center" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Correspondence to Zeyang Li (<span id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zeyang@mit.edu</span>).</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved remarkable success in controlling complex robotic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
However, the lack of safety guarantees still hinders the deployment of RL on real-world systems.
To mitigate this issue, safe RL aims to learn policies that achieve high rewards while satisfying safety constraints.
Predominant approaches use constrained policy optimization, such as Lagrangian-based methods, where constraints are typically imposed through either cost value functions or neural certificate functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
A key limitation of this line of work is that, although the learned policies may perform well empirically in some cases, they provide no formal guarantee of constraint satisfaction; as a result, catastrophic failures can still occur during deployment.
Another line of work in safe RL seeks to learn provably safe policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
These methods typically require verified explicit certificate functions representing control-invariant sets, such as control barrier function (CBF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> or safety index (SI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, whose synthesis and verification scale poorly with state dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, these certificate-based methods often yield overly conservative behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The backup control barrier function (BCBF) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> offers a promising way to avoid the fundamental difficulty of directly synthesizing a valid explicit control-invariant set.
Its key idea is to enlarge a small, known safe invariant set by forward-integrating a deterministic backup policy.
The implicit set induced by this rollout is control-invariant by construction, respects bounded actuator limits, and reduces the safety condition to relative degree one regardless of the underlying dynamics.
Thus, safety can be specified in its original form without high-order-style augmentations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
Crucially, this framework also preserves the convexity of the resulting safety-filter constraints, allowing them to be solved efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, BCBF shifts rather than eliminates the difficulty: it replaces the direct synthesis of an explicit control-invariant set with the design of a backup policy, but does not provide a principled way to obtain such a policy.
The quality of the backup policy critically affects the size of the resulting invariant set.
Hand-designed analytic policies, such as linear quadratic regulators (LQRs), are easy to certify but typically recover only a small portion of the full invariant set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
In this paper, we use BCBF as a building block and address this limitation by training the safe-arrival component of the backup policy via RL.
The learned policy steers the system into a small certified region around an equilibrium, where a closed-form linear controller takes over.
The BCBF construction then automatically extends safety from the certified base set to the entire implicit invariant set, without requiring certification of the learned safe-arrival policy itself.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14536v1/figures/PS2_Main_Figure.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/146;" width="476" height="146" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the PS2-RL framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PS2-RL (Provably Safe, <em id="S1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">yet</em> Scalable RL)</span>, a two-phase framework for training provably safe control policies without excessive conservatism or performance sacrifice.
A visual overview of our method is in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Phase I trains a safe-arrival policy with RL through a novel indicator-based safe-arrival value function, whose unique optimum induces the time-optimal policy that drives the system safely to a target set.
Phase II trains a PS2 policy <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">end-to-end</em> through a control-invariant layer (CIL), constructed from the learned backup policy in Phase I.
On unicycle lane-keeping and powerloop tracking for a <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional quadrotor, PS2-RL achieves <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> safety across both training and deployment while exceeding the performance of all baselines.
Our <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contributions</span> are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PS2-RL framework</span>:
A two-phase RL framework with formal safety guarantees and scalability to high-dimensional, input-constrained systems, without synthesizing an explicit invariant set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-arrival value function</span>:
A novel objective that encodes the time-optimal behavior of safely driving the system to a target set.
We show that it admits a self-consistency condition and a Bellman equation, enabling the training of a neural backup policy with standard RL pipelines.
The learned backup policy enlarges the implicit control-invariant set, empirically yielding substantially larger safe regions than analytic alternatives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Control-invariant layer</span>:
A differentiable projection layer that enforces the BCBF constraints, enabling efficient end-to-end training of provably safe RL policies.
Crucially, we show that the differentiable projection does not compromise the expressiveness of the policy network, preserving universal approximation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theoretical justifications</span>:
Comprehensive theoretical results for the proposed framework, including guarantees for each individual phase and their connection in the full PS2-RL pipeline.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related work.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We group related studies into five categories: (i) safe RL via constrained policy optimization, (ii) safe RL with verified certificate, (iii) backup control barrier functions, (iv) RL for reach-avoid specifications, and (v) differentiable optimization layers.
See App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Backup Control Barrier Functions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this work, we consider the following control-affine, continuous-time system:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{x}=f(x)+g(x)u,\quad x\in{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{n},\quad u\in{\mathcal{U}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{x}=f(x)+g(x)u,\quad x\in{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{n},\quad u\in{\mathcal{U}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the state, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state space, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is the control input, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact set of admissible inputs encoding actuator limits. The functions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> are locally Lipschitz continuous. Given a state-feedback policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\pi}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{R}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\pi}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{R}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed-loop flow map under <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\pi}(x):=f(x)+g(x)\pi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\pi}(x):=f(x)+g(x)\pi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\pi}(x_{0},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\pi}(x_{0},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the state at time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}:=x(t_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}:=x(t_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Safety of the system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is specified with a <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safe set</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}:=\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:h_{\mathcal{S}}(x)\geq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}:=\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:h_{\mathcal{S}}(x)\geq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> for a continuously differentiable <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\mathcal{S}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\mathcal{S}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and enforced in the sense of <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control invariance</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
Control barrier functions (CBFs) provide a differentiable certificate of control invariance via an inequality condition <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla h(x)^{\top}(f(x)+g(x)u)+\alpha(h(x))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla h(x)^{\top}(f(x)+g(x)u)+\alpha(h(x))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> enforced online within a safety filter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
The key challenge lies in the synthesis of a CBF whose zero-superlevel set is invariant.
As discussed in Sec. <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, classical tools for computing the explicit invariant set have various limitations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The backup control barrier function (BCBF) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> circumvents this by leveraging a deterministic <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">backup policy</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a small, known control-invariant <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">base set</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}:=\{x:h_{\mathcal{B}}(x)\geq 0\}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}:=\{x:h_{\mathcal{B}}(x)\geq 0\}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-time constrained backup-induced control-invariant set under <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Backup-induced Control-Invariant Set)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the set of all states from which <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers to <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the backup horizon <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping the trajectory in <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{C}}_{T}({\pi_{b}})=\mathcal{R}\left({\mathcal{B}},{\mathcal{S}},f_{\pi_{b}},T\right):=\left\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:{\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,T)\in{\mathcal{B}}\;\wedge{\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,t^{\prime})\in{\mathcal{S}}\quad\forall t^{\prime}\in[0,T]\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0.502em">∧</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{C}}_{T}({\pi_{b}})=\mathcal{R}\left({\mathcal{B}},{\mathcal{S}},f_{\pi_{b}},T\right):=\left\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:{\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,T)\in{\mathcal{B}}\;\wedge{\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,t^{\prime})\in{\mathcal{S}}\quad\forall t^{\prime}\in[0,T]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{T}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{T}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math> is control-invariant under <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}_{T}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}_{T}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and yields the BCBF, an implicit CBF:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(x)=\min\left\{\min_{t^{\prime}\in[0,T]}\left\{h_{\mathcal{S}}\left({\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,t^{\prime})\right)\right\},h_{\mathcal{B}}\left({\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,T)\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(x)=\min\left\{\min_{t^{\prime}\in[0,T]}\left\{h_{\mathcal{S}}\left({\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,t^{\prime})\right)\right\},h_{\mathcal{B}}\left({\Phi_{\pi_{b}}}(x,T)\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The non-smooth <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is handled by enforcing the CBF inequality condition pointwise along the backup flow and at the horizon.
The resulting BCBF constraints are sufficient for the original CBF condition on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\mathcal{C}_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\mathcal{C}_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Prop. 2]</cite> and are always feasible by construction under control limits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite>, without ever requiring an explicit invariant set computation, making BCBF an ideal backbone for PS2-RL.
We defer the explicit forms of CBF and BCBF constraints to Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a> and App. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Background on Safe Control Theory ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the control task as a Markov decision process (MDP) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{X}},{\mathcal{U}},F,r,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{X}},{\mathcal{U}},F,r,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> with deterministic transitions <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and discount factor <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A neural policy <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, maps states to control inputs.
The objective is to find a policy that maximizes expected discounted return <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J({\pi_{\phi}})=\mathbb{E}[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r(x_{k},u_{k})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J({\pi_{\phi}})=\mathbb{E}[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r(x_{k},u_{k})]</annotation></semantics></math> for an initial-state distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, under closed loop dynamics <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},{\pi_{\phi}}(x_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},{\pi_{\phi}}(x_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The state-action value function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\gamma}^{\pi_{\phi}}(x,u):=\mathbb{E}_{x,u}^{{\pi_{\phi}}}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r(x_{k},u_{k})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\gamma}^{\pi_{\phi}}(x,u):=\mathbb{E}_{x,u}^{{\pi_{\phi}}}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r(x_{k},u_{k})\right]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the self-consistency condition <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi_{\phi}}_{\gamma}(x,u)=r(x,u)+\gamma Q_{\gamma}^{\pi_{\phi}}(F(x,u),\,{\pi_{\phi}}(F(x,u)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi_{\phi}}_{\gamma}(x,u)=r(x,u)+\gamma Q_{\gamma}^{\pi_{\phi}}(F(x,u),\,{\pi_{\phi}}(F(x,u)))</annotation></semantics></math> for policy <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Hard-Constrained Neural Networks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A core requirement of PS2-RL is to enforce hard constraints on a neural network’s output, while preserving differentiability for end-to-end training.
We build on <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HardNet-CVX</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, which projects a neural network’s output onto a state-dependent convex set via a differentiable layer.
In our notation, we refer to this construction as the <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constraint projection layer</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Constraint Projection Layer)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an RL policy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a state-dependent convex set <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}(x)\subseteq{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}(x)\subseteq{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the constraint projection layer <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{P}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x)=\argmin_{u}\left\|u-{\pi_{\phi}}(x)\right\|_{2}^{2}\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad u\in{\mathbb{K}}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{P}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x)=\argmin_{u}\left\|u-{\pi_{\phi}}(x)\right\|_{2}^{2}\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad u\in{\mathbb{K}}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This projection is computed by a differentiable convex optimization layer, allowing policy gradients to propagate through <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> during end-to-end training.
Crucially, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the universal approximation property of <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HardNet-CVX</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, Thm 12]</cite>.
In Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, we instantiate <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with affine constraints derived from the BCBF framework to define PS2-RL’s <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control-invariant layer</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now formalize our main problem: synthesize a reward-maximizing RL policy that is provably safe under control limits for every initial state of interest.
First, we make the following connection:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is continuous time, while the RL policy is evaluated at sample times <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}=k\,{\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}=k\,{\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> and its output is held by zero-order hold (ZOH).
Accordingly, the transition map <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Reinforcement Learning ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> represents the exact ZOH dynamics of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) over one sampling interval.
Our theoretical guarantees are for this sampled-data discrete dynamics.
Under standard Lipschitz regularity and a sufficiently fast control rate, the sampled-data set-invariance conditions guarantee that BCBF conditions enforced at sample instants imply continuous-time safety of the ZOH trajectory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With this connection between continuous- and discrete-time established, we present our main problem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproblem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproblem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1</span></span><span id="Thmproblem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PS2-RL Formulation)<span id="Thmproblem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproblem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproblem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproblem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the control-affine system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and its ZOH discretization <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, a safe set <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\subseteq{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\subseteq{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, an admissible control input set <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, an initial-state distribution <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> supported on <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a reward function <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for task performance, and a discount factor <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, find the policy <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that solves</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad J(\pi)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{0}\sim\rho_{0},\;u_{k}=\pi(x_{k})\\
x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})\end{subarray}}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\,r(x_{k},u_{k})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad J(\pi)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{0}\sim\rho_{0},\;u_{k}=\pi(x_{k})\\
x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})\end{subarray}}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\,r(x_{k},u_{k})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}},\quad u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}},\quad\forall k\geq 0,\;\;\forall x_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}},\quad u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}},\quad\forall k\geq 0,\;\;\forall x_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the feasible policy class of Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which constrains each realized control input <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the resulting state <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the requirements <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> must hold pointwise in <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, for every trajectory induced by <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and for every initial state in <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We require the learned policy for Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to be provably safe, i.e., to provably belong to <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that predominant safe RL algorithms based on constrained policy optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> may adopt similar formulations, such as state-wise safe RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.
However, the policies learned by these methods generally do not come with formal guarantees of membership in <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Provably Safe, <em id="S4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">yet</em> Scalable RL</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> requires maximizing task return while guaranteeing safety under bounded control.
PS2-RL decomposes this into two subproblems (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
Then, we first train a safe-arrival policy using the safe-arrival value function to quickly drive the system to a certified base set while avoiding unsafe states (Secs. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Phase I: Training the Safe-Arrival Policy ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).
Second, the resulting composed backup policy instantiates the control-invariant layer, a differentiable projection layer mapping inputs to the BCBF-admissible set.
This enables end-to-end training of an RL policy through the safety constraints (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).
By enlarging the implicit invariant set before optimizing within it, PS2-RL achieves both high returns and guaranteed safety.
All proofs are deferred to the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We decompose Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> into two subproblems: a BCBF-constrained policy optimization for a fixed backup policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and horizon <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a>) and a safe-arrival set maximization problem for the backup policy itself (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.2</span></a>), and show their composition certifies an inner approximation of Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="4.1.3 Certified decomposition guarantee ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy</h4>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">BCBF-admissible input set.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,t)=\frac{\partial\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,t)}{\partial x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,t)=\frac{\partial\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,t)}{\partial x}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the sensitivity of the backup flow starting at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
The BCBF derivative terms at the current state under candidate input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> are as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{D}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\mathcal{S}}(x,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{D}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\mathcal{S}}(x,u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla h_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)^{\top}\left[\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\left(f(x)+g(x)u\right)-f_{\pi_{b}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla h_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)^{\top}\left[\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\left(f(x)+g(x)u\right)-f_{\pi_{b}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{D}}_{T}^{\mathcal{B}}(x,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{D}}_{T}^{\mathcal{B}}(x,u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla h_{\mathcal{B}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\right)^{\top}\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\left(f(x)+g(x)u\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla h_{\mathcal{B}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\right)^{\top}\Psi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\left(f(x)+g(x)u\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given extended class-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the BCBF-admissible input set is</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbb{K}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}}):=\big\{u\in{\mathcal{U}}\,:\,&{\mathcal{D}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\mathcal{S}}(x,u)+\alpha_{\mathcal{S}}\left(h_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)\right)\geq 0\quad{t^{\prime}}\in[0,T],\\
&{\mathcal{D}}_{T}^{\mathcal{B}}(x,u)+\alpha_{\mathcal{B}}\left(h_{\mathcal{B}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\right)\right)\geq 0\big\}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0.618em">:</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2.000em;" width="2.000em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbb{K}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}}):=\big\{u\in{\mathcal{U}}\,:\,&amp;{\mathcal{D}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\mathcal{S}}(x,u)+\alpha_{\mathcal{S}}\left(h_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,{t^{\prime}})\right)\right)\geq 0\quad{t^{\prime}}\in[0,T],\\
&amp;{\mathcal{D}}_{T}^{\mathcal{B}}(x,u)+\alpha_{\mathcal{B}}\left(h_{\mathcal{B}}\left(\Phi_{\pi_{b}}(x,T)\right)\right)\geq 0\big\}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, these constraints are affine in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> since the backup rollout and sensitivity are computed under a fixed <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math> is convex whenever <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex.
Moreover, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible for (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite>.
With the BCBF-admissible set, we present our first subproblem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsubproblem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_subproblem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsubproblem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subproblem 1</span></span><span id="Thmsubproblem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization)<span id="Thmsubproblem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsubproblem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a backup policy <math id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> and backup horizon <math id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume <math id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Find a <math id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>-parameterized policy <math id="Thmsubproblem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the RL objective while satisfying the BCBF-constraints:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi_{\phi}}\quad J({\pi_{\phi}})\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\pi_{\phi}(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}})\quad\forall x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi_{\phi}}\quad J({\pi_{\phi}})\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\pi_{\phi}(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}})\quad\forall x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In practice, we enforce the constraints on a finite relative-time mesh <math id="S4.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=t^{\prime}_{0}&lt;t^{\prime}_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;t^{\prime}_{N}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=t^{\prime}_{0}&lt;t^{\prime}_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;t^{\prime}_{N}=T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under standard Lipschitz regularity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and Remark <a href="#S3.Thmremark1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, sampled BCBF enforcement at instants <math id="S4.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> implies continuous-time safety of the ZOH trajectory.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conservatism in Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is governed by the backup-induced set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(\pi_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(\pi_{b})</annotation></semantics></math>:
a small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(\pi_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}(\pi_{b})</annotation></semantics></math> gives the policy a narrow region in which to optimize.
To enlarge this region without losing the BCBF guarantee, we anchor the construction of the backup policy with a certified base set and use a learnable safe-arrival policy only to steer states to that base set, as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\pi_{b}}(x)=\begin{cases}\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x),&amp;x\in{\mathcal{B}}\\
{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x),&amp;x\notin{\mathcal{B}}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\pi_{b}}(x)=\begin{cases}\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x),&amp;x\in{\mathcal{B}}\\
{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x),&amp;x\notin{\mathcal{B}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{B}}:{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{B}}:{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">base controller</span>, which renders the base set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> forward invariant under <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\pi_{\mathcal{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\pi_{\mathcal{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}:{\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}:{\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safe-arrival policy</span>, which is tasked with steering the system from a state outside <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the backup horizon <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> while staying in the safe set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
While the terminal pair <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be certified for forward invariance, the safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Certified base set and base controller.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Constructing a valid <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prerequisite for a BCBF, and thus also for our PS2-RL framework.
Here, we show that this construction is always possible and tractable for nonlinear dynamical systems under a mild condition.
Crucially, this does not require computationally expensive synthesis or global search.
Intuitively, constructing a small local invariant set around an equilibrium is much easier than synthesizing a globally valid explicit invariant set, which is often intractable for high-dimensional systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Local Stabilizability)<span id="S4.Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}\in\mathrm{int}({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}\in\mathrm{int}({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\star}\in\mathrm{int}({\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\star}\in\mathrm{int}({\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that: (a) <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\star},u^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\star},u^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> is an equilibrium of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), i.e., <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x^{\star})+g(x^{\star})u^{\star}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x^{\star})+g(x^{\star})u^{\star}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; and (b) the linearization <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Backup Control Barrier Functions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) about <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\star},u^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\star},u^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\frac{\partial(f+gu^{\star})}{\partial x}\big|_{x=x^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\frac{\partial(f+gu^{\star})}{\partial x}\big|_{x=x^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmassumption1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=g(x^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=g(x^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>, is locally stabilizable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Local Stabilizability). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> is standard in nonlinear control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and is satisfied by any continuously differentiable system in a neighborhood of an equilibrium <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\star},u^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\star},u^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> that admits first-order-controllable linearization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Existence of a Certified Local Base Set)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Local Stabilizability). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, there exists a linear feedback gain <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, a symmetric positive-definite matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a constant <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the base controller <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x)=u^{\star}-K(x-x^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x)=u^{\star}-K(x-x^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> and the sublevel set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{c}=\left\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:(x-x^{\star})^{\top}P(x-x^{\star})\leq c\right\},\,c\in(0,\bar{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{c}=\left\{x\in{\mathcal{X}}:(x-x^{\star})^{\top}P(x-x^{\star})\leq c\right\},\,c\in(0,\bar{c}]</annotation></semantics></math>
together satisfy the following properties: (i) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{c}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{c}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>; (ii) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x)\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x)\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>; (iii) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is control-invariant under <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and (iv) every trajectory with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(0)\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(0)\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)\to x^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)\to x^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)\in{\mathcal{B}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> shows that any stabilizing linear feedback <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., pole placement, LQR, etc.) followed by a sublevel-set check for input feasibility and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-containment (i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>) produces a valid <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hereafter, we fix one valid choice <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> for the base set and base controller produced by Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math> is governed by the safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the backup horizon <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
While <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math> monotonically increases with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in the set inclusion sense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Lemma 1]</cite>, it also increases the number of constraints in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}</annotation></semantics></math> and could render the optimization problem inefficient.
A more straightforward solution is to come up with a better <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that can bring more states in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> given a fixed horizon.
To this end, we introduce our second subproblem, learning a safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">safe-arrival set</span>, and thereby the BCBF-induced invariant set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsubproblem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_subproblem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsubproblem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subproblem 2</span></span><span id="Thmsubproblem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Safe-Arrival Set Maximization)<span id="Thmsubproblem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsubproblem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a certified base set <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and base controller <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> from Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, a backup horizon <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a compact design region <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subseteq{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a finite reference measure <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, find a <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>-parameterized safe-arrival policy <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}:{\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}:{\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the measure of states in <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> from which it can safely arrive at <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="Thmsubproblem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}}\quad\mu_{\mathrm{SA}}\left({\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}};\,\Omega,\,{\mathcal{B}}\right):=\mu\!\left({\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\theta}})\cap\left(\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\right)\right)\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad{\pi_{b}^{\theta}}(x)=\begin{cases}\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x),&amp;x\in{\mathcal{B}},\\
{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}(x),&amp;x\notin{\mathcal{B}}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.433em;" width="0.433em"><mi>μ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}}\quad\mu_{\mathrm{SA}}\left({\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}};\,\Omega,\,{\mathcal{B}}\right):=\mu\!\left({\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\theta}})\cap\left(\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}\right)\right)\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad{\pi_{b}^{\theta}}(x)=\begin{cases}\pi_{\mathcal{B}}(x),&amp;x\in{\mathcal{B}},\\
{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}(x),&amp;x\notin{\mathcal{B}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span>Certified decomposition guarantee</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem2" title="Subproblem 2 (Safe-Arrival Set Maximization). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> enlarges the backup-induced invariant set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> over which safety is guaranteed, and motivates the safe-arrival value formulation (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> then optimizes the task return inside the corresponding BCBF-admissible input set (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
Accordingly, for a fixed backup policy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the BCBF-feasible policy class as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}}):=\{\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}:\pi(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}(x;{\pi_{b}})\,\,\forall x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.507em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}}):=\{\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}:\pi(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}(x;{\pi_{b}})\,\,\forall x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result formalizes this decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Certified Decomposition and Exactness of PS2-RL)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a solution of Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem2" title="Subproblem 2 (Safe-Arrival Set Maximization). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a solution of Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with the fixed backup policy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the exact BCBF constraints in (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) are enforced. Then:
(i) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}\left({\pi_{b}^{\star}}\right)\subseteq\Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}\left({\pi_{b}^{\star}}\right)\subseteq\Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible for Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>;
(ii) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal among all policies certified by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J({\pi_{\phi}^{\star}})=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})}J(\pi)\leq\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}}J(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J({\pi_{\phi}^{\star}})=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})}J(\pi)\leq\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}}J(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>;
and (iii) if there exists a globally optimal solution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>safe</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> of Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{safe}}\in\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}\left({\pi_{b}^{\star}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>safe</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{safe}}\in\Pi_{\mathrm{BCBF}}\left({\pi_{b}^{\star}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also globally optimal for Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Thus, the two subproblems solve a certified inner approximation of Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. They solve Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> exactly whenever the learned backup policy induces a BCBF-feasible class containing an optimal safe policy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Safe-Arrival Value Function</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem2" title="Subproblem 2 (Safe-Arrival Set Maximization). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> optimizes the backup policy to maximize the size of the induced implicit invariant set, thereby imposing fewer restrictions on the RL policy in Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and enabling potentially higher rewards.
To this end, we introduce the safe-arrival value function, a novel indicator-style objective that quantifies a policy’s ability to safely return the system to the target set.
Optimizing the safe-arrival value function encourages the policy to reach <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> quickly while avoiding the failure set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}:={\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}:={\mathcal{X}}\setminus{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby enlarging <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}(x):=\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{B}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}(x):=\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{B}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}(x):=\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{F}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}(x):=\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{F}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}(x):=1-\mathbf{b}(x)-\mathbf{f}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}(x):=1-\mathbf{b}(x)-\mathbf{f}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> precisely on the continuation set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\setminus({\mathcal{B}}\cup{\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\setminus({\mathcal{B}}\cup{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to the policy optimized for this purpose as the safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Safe-Arrival Value Function)<span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a deterministic safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and discount factor <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the discounted safe-arrival Q-function is:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x,u):=\mathbb{E}_{x,u}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\beta^{k}\left(\prod_{\tau=0}^{k-1}\mathbf{c}(x_{\tau})\right)\mathbf{b}(x_{k})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x,u):=\mathbb{E}_{x,u}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\beta^{k}\left(\prod_{\tau=0}^{k-1}\mathbf{c}(x_{\tau})\right)\mathbf{b}(x_{k})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=x,u_{0}=u,x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=x,u_{0}=u,x_{k+1}=F(x_{k},u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}={\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}={\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the empty product interpreted as <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The state-value function is <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x):=Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x,{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x):=Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x,{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The factor <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{\tau=0}^{k-1}\mathbf{c}(x_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{\tau=0}^{k-1}\mathbf{c}(x_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> is a “survival” gate: it is <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> only while the rollout remains in the continuation set, and becomes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> immediately after the trajectory enters either <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In Definition 4.1 (Safe-Arrival Value Function). ‣ 4.2 Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) rewards exactly the first safe arrival to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, discounted by how many steps it takes.
Under deterministic dynamics, (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In Definition 4.1 (Safe-Arrival Value Function). ‣ 4.2 Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) equals <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if the rollout reaches <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> safely in exactly <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Consequently, maximizing the safe-arrival value function trains a policy that first prefers safe arrival over failure, and among safe-arrival policies prefers those that reach <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> sooner.
Importantly, safe-arrival value satisfies the following self-consistency condition:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}}(x,u)=\mathbf{b}(x)+\beta\,\mathbf{c}(x)\,Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}}\left(F(x,u),{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}\left(F(x,u)\right)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}}(x,u)=\mathbf{b}(x)+\beta\,\mathbf{c}(x)\,Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}}\left(F(x,u),{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}}\left(F(x,u)\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">as well as a corresponding Bellman equation.
This structure enables the safe-arrival policy to be trained using standard RL pipelines.
The full details and additional properties of the safe-arrival value function are deferred to App. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Phase I: Training the Safe-Arrival Policy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Phase I addresses Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem2" title="Subproblem 2 (Safe-Arrival Set Maximization). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, left panel) by training a parameterized safe-arrival policy <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the discounted Bellman recursion (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2 Safe-Arrival Value Function ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
Letting <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{arr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>arr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{arr}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a design distribution supported on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\setminus{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we optimize the following objective:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta}\;J_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\rho_{\mathrm{arr}}}\!\left[Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}}(x,{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}(x))\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>arr</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>SA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}\;J_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\rho_{\mathrm{arr}}}\!\left[Q_{\mathrm{SA},\beta}^{{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}}(x,{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}(x))\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The general Phase I training procedure and algorithm are in App. <a href="#A5.SS6" title="E.6 Phase I: Safe-Arrival Policy Training ‣ Appendix E PS2-RL Details and Proofs ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>.
Note that the only ingredients specific for the safe-arrival policy training are the indicators <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b},\mathbf{f},\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b},\mathbf{f},\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, first-hit termination at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.3 Phase I: Training the Safe-Arrival Policy ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
Everything else can be supplied by a chosen RL backbone.
Hence, the safe-arrival policy training can be instantiated with actor-critic, Q-learning, and related methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Phase II solves Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, right panel).
With <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the safe-arrival policy trained from Phase I, we define the fixed composed backup policy by setting <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}^{\star}(x)=\pi_{B}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}^{\star}(x)=\pi_{B}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}^{\star}(x)=\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{SA}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}^{\star}(x)=\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{SA}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
With <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, the backup-induced invariant set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the BCBF-admissible set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed as well. Assuming <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> is convex for each <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> because the BCBF constraints are affine in <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> under a fixed backup flow.
Extending from the constraint projection layer (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Constraint Projection Layer). ‣ 2.3 Hard-Constrained Neural Networks ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), we define the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control-invariant layer</span> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Control-Invariant Layer)<span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the fixed composed backup policy <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> and backup horizon <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the control-invariant layer is the state-dependent projection operator</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x):=\argmin_{u}\,\|u-{\pi_{\phi}}(x)\|_{2}^{2}\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad u\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>CIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x):=\argmin_{u}\,\|u-{\pi_{\phi}}(x)\|_{2}^{2}\quad\mathrm{s.t.}\quad u\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Definition 4.2 (Control-Invariant Layer). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) is essentially the BCBF-QP written as a differentiable projection layer.
Note that for a relative-time mesh of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N+1</annotation></semantics></math> nodes (Remark <a href="#S4.Thmremark1" title="Remark 4.1. ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), this QP has at least <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N+2+2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N+2+2m</annotation></semantics></math> affine constraints in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. The constraint count thus grows linearly with the backup horizon, which precludes a closed-form solution and motivates the usage of a differentiable QP solver (App. <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 Phase II: Control-Invariant Layer and PS2 Policy ‣ Appendix E PS2-RL Details and Proofs ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>).
We now define our PS2 policy:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PS2 Policy)<span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}:{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the PS2 policy is</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}}):={\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>PS2</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>CIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}}):={\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}})(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This reparameterization turns Subproblem <a href="#Thmsubproblem1" title="Subproblem 1 (BCBF-Constrained Policy Optimization). ‣ BCBF-admissible input set. ‣ 4.1.1 BCBF-constrained policies under a fixed backup policy ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> into unconstrained optimization over policy parameters:
<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\phi}J(\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}(\cdot;{\pi_{b}^{\star}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>PS2</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\phi}J(\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}(\cdot;{\pi_{b}^{\star}}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because the projection layer is differentiable, gradients propagate end-to-end through (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Definition 4.2 (Control-Invariant Layer). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Hence Phase II is agnostic to the RL backbone with the only architectural modification being the control-invariant layer appended to the policy output.
Regardless of the stochasticity of the policy output, the projection is applied deterministically.
Therefore, PS2-RL guarantees safety in both training and deployment, and is expressive as shown in the following theoretical results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Safety Guarantee of PS2-RL)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed composed backup policy, and assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{0}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any policy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding PS2 policy satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}\in\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>PS2</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}\in\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Certified Decomposition and Exactness of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.1.3 Certified decomposition guarantee ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}\in\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>PS2</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\phi}_{\mathrm{PS2}}\in\Pi_{{\mathrm{safe}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and is therefore feasible for Problem <a href="#Thmproblem1" title="Problem 1 (PS2-RL Formulation). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Every realized input satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the sampled closed-loop trajectory satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}\in{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under the sampled-data conditions, the continuous-time ZOH trajectory remains in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Universal Approximation of PS2-RL)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{b}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{b}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>, assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subseteq{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> be compact.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of continuous maps <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> with norm <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\pi\|_{\infty}:=\sup_{x\in\Omega}\|\pi(x)\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\pi\|_{\infty}:=\sup_{x\in\Omega}\|\pi(x)\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For function classes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN},\mathcal{G}\subseteq C(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN},\mathcal{G}\subseteq C(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}</annotation></semantics></math> universally approximates <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
for every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}\in\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}\in\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\pi-{\pi_{\phi}}\|_{\infty}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\pi-{\pi_{\phi}}\|_{\infty}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the class of PS2 policies obtained by composing (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Definition 4.2 (Control-Invariant Layer). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}</annotation></semantics></math>-class policies as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{PS2}^{\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega):=\left\{{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}}):{\pi_{\phi}}\in\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>CIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{PS2}^{\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega):=\left\{{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathrm{CIL}}({\pi_{\phi}}):{\pi_{\phi}}\in\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the BCBF-feasible policy class on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega):=\left\{\pi\in\mathcal{G}:\pi(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})\quad\forall x\in\Omega\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega):=\left\{\pi\in\mathcal{G}:\pi(x)\in{\mathbb{K}}_{\mathrm{BCBF}}(x;{\pi_{b}^{\star}})\quad\forall x\in\Omega\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{PS2}^{\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>NN</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{PS2}^{\mathcal{G}_{\rm NN}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> universally
approximates <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>BCBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathrm{BCBF}}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}};\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">The same statement holds in </span><math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> by the corresponding </span><math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> version of </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="footnote1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a><span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, Thm. 12]</span></cite><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, where </span><math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}(\Omega,{\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> denotes </span><math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-integrable maps with norm </span><math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\pi\|_{p}=(\int_{\Omega}\|\pi(x)\|_{p}^{p}dx)^{1/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\pi\|_{p}=(\int_{\Omega}\|\pi(x)\|_{p}^{p}dx)^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Together, Thms. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Safety Guarantee of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Universal Approximation of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> show that the CIL enforces safety without sacrificing expressivity.
Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Safety Guarantee of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> guarantees that every projected policy is BCBF-feasible and hence safe, while Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Universal Approximation of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> shows that the projection preserves the approximation power of the chosen policy architecture over the BCBF-feasible class.
Thus, PS2-RL combines hard safety guarantees with the policy expressivity needed for high-performance RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Scalability of PS2-RL</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The scalability of PS2-RL comes from reducing provably safe RL to rollout-based operations and a control-space projection, rather than a global invariant set computation.
From a practical standpoint, PS2-RL is simple to instantiate: <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be a distance-to-obstacle margin, and the only component that must be explicitly certified is the local base pair <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{B}},\pi_{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is easily obtained via Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Existence of a Certified Local Base Set). ‣ Certified base set and base controller. ‣ 4.1.2 Learning the safe-arrival policy to enlarge the implicit invariant set ‣ 4.1 Decomposing Safety and Performance from Problem ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
The learned safe-arrival policy need not be certified separately, as once it is fixed, our framework extends the base set to the rollout-induced invariant set <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{T}}({\pi_{b}^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> by construction, avoiding direct synthesis or verification of a global invariant set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the CIL remains small in decision dimension even when the state is high-dimensional.
With <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> backup nodes, <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> safety constraints, and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> terminal constraints, the CIL has <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> control variables and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Nn_{\mathcal{S}}+n_{\mathcal{B}}+2m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Nn_{\mathcal{S}}+n_{\mathcal{B}}+2m)</annotation></semantics></math> affine rows.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            For the explicit QP construction, see App. <a href="#A5.SS7.SSS1" title="E.7.1 Control-Invariant Layer Construction and Solver ‣ E.7 Phase II: Control-Invariant Layer and PS2 Policy ‣ Appendix E PS2-RL Details and Proofs ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7.1</span></a>.</span></span></span>
Thus, the number of constraints grows only linearly with the backup mesh size or the system dimension.
In contrast, computing the global invariant set by discretizing the Hamilton-Jacobi reachability PDE incurs complexity that grows exponentially with the state dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, PS2-RL is agnostic to the RL backbone and the policy architecture: the CIL is appended after the output of <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Thm. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Universal Approximation of PS2-RL). ‣ 4.4 Phase II: Training with the Control-Invariant Layer ‣ 4 Provably Safe, yet Scalable RL ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> shows that this projection preserves universal approximation over feasible policies.
Thus, unlike post-hoc DNN verification methods that are architecture-dependent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, PS2-RL is not limited to a specific network family.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, although our presentation uses an analytic control-affine model, PS2-RL does not fundamentally rely on a hand-derived symbolic decomposition <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)+g(x)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)+g(x)u</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, given a differentiable simulator, backup rollouts and the sensitivities needed by the CIL can be computed by automatic differentiation for only the safety-relevant outputs.
The differentiable-simulator variant is further discussed in App. <a href="#A5.SS5" title="E.5 Constructing the CIL from Differentiable Simulators ‣ Appendix E PS2-RL Details and Proofs ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate PS2-RL on two tracking tasks whose reference trajectories intentionally leave the safe set. The first is a low-dimensional unicycle lane-keeping task, where dense grid evaluation lets us visualize how the learned safe-arrival policy enlarges the backup-induced invariant set. The second is an agile <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional quadrotor powerloop task with body rate and thrust as control, which tests scalability under tight actuation and a high-relative-degree altitude constraint. In both cases, the reward is a negative weighted tracking cost.
Full details and extended analyses are provided in App. <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Unicycle Environment Details ‣ Appendix F Experiment Details ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a> and App. <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Unicycle: Extended Analysis ‣ Appendix G Additional Results, Ablation Studies, and Computation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a> for the unicycle environment, and App. <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 Quadrotor Environment Details ‣ Appendix F Experiment Details ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a> and App. <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Quadrotor: Extended Analysis ‣ Appendix G Additional Results, Ablation Studies, and Computation ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a> for the quadrotor.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines and protocol.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare PS2<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SA</span></sub> (PS2+<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>) against seven baselines: (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pen</span>) low/high-penalty SAC; (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Con</span>) CPO and SAC-Lag. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>; and (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PSRL</span>) CBF-RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, MPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and PS2<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ABP</span></sub>, where PS2<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ABP</span></sub> replaces the learned safe-arrival policy with an analytic one.
CBF-RL uses a sum-of-squares CBF for unicycle and an HOCBF for quadrotor, and the filter is used for training but removed at evaluation, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. We give MPS a longer recovery horizon than PS2-RL.
Tables <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Unicycle: Lane-Keeping ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Quadrotor: Powerloop Maneuver ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> report sampled-data metrics under each method’s controller.
All methods are trained with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> seeds and evaluated on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> episodes per seed, and we report the IQM with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> CIs and the aggregated safety.
See App. <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 PS2-RL Implementation Details ‣ Appendix F Experiment Details ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a> for PS2-RL implementation details and App. <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Baseline Implementation Details ‣ Appendix F Experiment Details ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> for the baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Unicycle: Lane-Keeping</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The unicycle state is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=[y,v,\psi]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=[y,v,\psi]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and inputs are <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mathrm{cmd}}\in[-5,5]\,\mathrm{m/s^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mathrm{cmd}}\in[-5,5]\,\mathrm{m/s^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{cmd}}\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{cmd}}\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> rad/s. The reference is a 20 sec sinusoid with amplitude <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math> m and desired speed <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{des}}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>des</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{des}}=5</annotation></semantics></math> m/s, while the safe set is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}=\{x:|y|\leq 1.8,\;|\psi|\leq\pi/3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mn>1.8</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}=\{x:|y|\leq 1.8,\;|\psi|\leq\pi/3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The base set is an LQR-induced ellipsoid about <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}=[0,v_{\mathrm{des}},0]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>des</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}=[0,v_{\mathrm{des}},0]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Dense safe-arrival evaluation on a <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="201\times 121\times 201" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>201</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>121</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>201</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">201\times 121\times 201</annotation></semantics></math> grid shows that the learned <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>SA</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\mathrm{SA}}^{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> enlarges the safe-arrival measure <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{SA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>SA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{SA}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.23</annotation></semantics></math> for the analytic policy to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.33</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.33</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1 Unicycle: Lane-Keeping ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> visualizes this larger induced set and the resulting less-conservative tracking behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14536v1/figures/unicycle_traj.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/123;" width="476" height="123" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Unicycle lane-keeping. The learned safe-arrival policy enlarges the backup-induced invariant set compared to the one induced by the analytic policy, enabling PS2<sub id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.F2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SA</span></sub> to track closer to the intentionally unsafe reference while remaining inside the lane constraints.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Unicycle: Lane-Keeping ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe, Yet ScalableReinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that PS2<sub id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SA</span></sub> achieves <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> safety with the best lateral and heading tracking among methods that are also <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> safe. Compared with PS2<sub id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ABP</span></sub>, the learned policy reduces lateral RMSE from <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.97</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.97</annotation></semantics></math> m to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.52</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.52</annotation></semantics></math> m, showing that Phase I directly reduces downstream conservatism.
The higher velocity RMSE of PS2<sub id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SA</span></sub> is consistent with the task objective, which prioritizes lateral tracking over speed tracking through a larger reward weight.
PS2<sub id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SA</span></sub> therefore trades speed accuracy for much lower lateral and heading errors.
SAC-Pen<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>high</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{high}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also safe, but is substantially more conservative, while the low-penalty and CMDP baselines achieve tracking only by allowing frequent violations. CBF-RL and MPS have formal safety mechanisms, but under the evaluated sampled-data deployment they are not uniformly safe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Unicycle lane-keeping experiment results across models trained with 10 different seeds.
</figcaption>
